Do not abort when the ball model is changed.

Changing the ball model killed the title: `assertion failed: i >= 0 && i < a->elem_num,
share/array.c:143`, and an assert here is `abort` - the game is gone, with one line of log
and no menu to return to.

`set_curr_ball` indexes the scanned list with `curr_ball` and nothing checks it first.
There are two ways for that index to be wrong, and neither is reachable on a desktop,
which is why upstream has no guard:

  - `init_balls` assigns `curr_ball` only when a scanned path matches the configured one.
    A scan that comes back shorter than the last one - or empty, if `fs_dir_scan` fails -
    leaves the previous index in a file-scope static.
  - `ball_action`'s wrap tests `++curr_ball == array_len(balls)`, which steps straight over
    the end rather than clamping if the index was already past it.

So the index is checked before it indexes. An out-of-range one is reported and reset to
the first model; an empty list is reported and the change refused, because an empty list
means the ball `.sol` files are missing from the packaged data and that is a build problem
this screen would otherwise hide.

**This does not say what made the index wrong on hardware.** The console has
`basic-ball-solid.sol` and the filter needs only one of the three, so the list should not
be empty - the log line will say which case it actually is on the next run. The crash is
worth removing either way: a menu that aborts the game is worse than any answer it could
give.

diff --git a/ball/st_ball.c b/ball/st_ball.c
index 145609c..b606810 100644
--- a/ball/st_ball.c
+++ b/ball/st_ball.c
@@ -24,6 +24,7 @@
 #include "audio.h"
 #include "geom.h"
 #include "video.h"
+#include "log.h" /* log_printf - see set_curr_ball */
 #include "demo.h"
 
 #include "game_server.h"
@@ -108,6 +109,38 @@ static void free_balls(void)
 
 static void set_curr_ball(void)
 {
+    /*
+     * **The index is checked before it indexes.** `array_get` asserts, and an assert on a
+     * console is `abort` - the game is gone, with one line of log and no menu to go back to.
+     * Changing the ball model did exactly that.
+     *
+     * Two ways in. `init_balls` only assigns `curr_ball` when a scanned path matches the
+     * configured one, so a scan that comes back shorter than last time - or empty, if
+     * `fs_dir_scan` fails - leaves a stale index behind; and `ball_action`'s wrap tests
+     * `== array_len(balls)`, which steps straight over the end if `curr_ball` was already past
+     * it. Neither is reachable on a desktop where the scan always succeeds, which is why
+     * upstream has no guard.
+     *
+     * Reported rather than silently clamped, because an empty list means the ball `.sol` files
+     * are missing from the packaged data, and that is a build problem this screen would
+     * otherwise hide.
+     */
+    const int n = balls ? array_len(balls) : 0;
+
+    if (n <= 0)
+    {
+        log_printf("ball: no ball models found - the list is empty, "
+                   "so the model cannot be changed\n");
+        return;
+    }
+
+    if (curr_ball >= n || curr_ball < 0)
+    {
+        log_printf("ball: index %d is outside the %d model(s) found; using the first\n",
+                   curr_ball, n);
+        curr_ball = 0;
+    }
+
     sprintf(ball_file, "%s/%s",
             DIR_ITEM_GET(balls, curr_ball)->path,
             base_name(DIR_ITEM_GET(balls, curr_ball)->path));
